const title = 'Gut Health Checklist: My Daily Habits for a Healthy Gut and Glowing Skin';
const description = 'My simple gut health checklist: eat enough, get protein, eat plants in different colors, walk after meals, hydrate sensibly, sleep and calm your stress. Small daily habits for better digestion and glowing skin.';
const faq = [
  ['How can I improve my gut health?', 'I focus on the basics: regular meals, enough protein, a variety of plants and fibre, healthy fats, gentle movement, sleep and stress care. Small habits repeated for months work better than a strict protocol, and persistent digestive symptoms deserve a doctor’s opinion.'],
  ['Does walking after meals help digestion?', 'A gentle walk after eating may support normal gut motility and post-meal blood sugar, and some people feel less heavy afterwards. Trials show that even 10 to 15 minutes after meals can help blood sugar, mostly in people with or at risk of impaired glucose tolerance, and it does not need to be intense.'],
  ['Does drinking more water make skin glow?', 'A systematic review of six studies found that extra water was linked with a slight rise in skin hydration, mainly in people who drank little to begin with, and the authors called the evidence weak. So I drink to thirst and activity, eat water-rich foods and use a good moisturiser.'],
  ['Does stress affect gut health and skin?', 'It can. Stress may change gut motility and digestion, and stress hormones can impair the skin barrier, which may make acne, redness or sensitivity feel worse for some people.'],
  ['Do I need to eat “clean” for a healthy gut?', 'No. Your microbiome generally benefits from variety, not perfection. Adding plants, fibre and fermented foods usually helps more than cutting everything out, and bread, pasta and dessert can still fit a nourishing diet.'],
  ['How many plants should I eat a week?', 'There is no magic number, but data from a large citizen-science microbiome project found that people who ate around 30 different plant foods a week had more diverse gut microbes than those who ate 10 or fewer. I use it as a fun goal: herbs, seeds, nuts, legumes and spices all count.'],
];
