<div class="art-head"><div><div class="up" style="color:#8A8A8A">Notes · Habits · Gut and skin, part 4 · Updated October 2026</div><h1 class="hd">Gut Health Checklist: My Daily Habits for Glowing Skin</h1><p class="serif stand">Glowing skin and better digestion are often less about adding more, and more about the tiny daily habits you repeat consistently. This is part 4 of my skin barrier and gut health series: how I eat, drink, move and rest, the evidence behind each habit, and the checklist I come back to.</p><div class="hand" style="transform:rotate(-2deg);font-size:34px;margin-top:10px">boring little habits, repeated</div></div>
<img class="art-hero" src="/img/curlers-selfie-hero.webp" alt="Selfie in a white robe with pink hair curlers and a pink hydrogel patch on the cheek" style="width:100%;max-width:1240px;height:auto;aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover;display:block;margin:0 auto"></div></section>
<section class="p art-grid"><div class="art-body">

<p>After the first three parts of this series, the natural question is: <strong>what does a normal day look like?</strong> My answer is simple. Healthy skin and digestion are usually built from boring little habits repeated for months, not from a perfect protocol.</p>
<p>This note is my practical gut health checklist, with what research says about each habit and where the evidence is thin. It is general information from my own reading and routine, not medical advice, and everyone’s body is different.</p>

<h2>Key takeaways</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Digestion starts before food reaches your stomach, so slow down a little.</li>
<li>Regular meals, enough calories, enough protein and healthy fats give your skin what it needs for repair.</li>
<li>Hydration is more than drinking a lot of water, and extra water helps most when you were drinking too little.</li>
<li>A gentle 10 to 15 minute walk after meals is one of the easiest habits, with good evidence for blood sugar.</li>
<li>Sleep and stress affect both your gut and your skin.</li>
<li>A gut-friendly diet means variety, not “cleaner and cleaner”.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Your digestion starts before food reaches your stomach</h2>
<p>Digestion begins in your head and mouth, and your nervous system is part of it. A few small things help me:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>Eat a little slower.</strong> Chewing is the first step of digestion and mechanically breaks food down before it reaches your stomach.</li>
<li><strong>Sit down when you can.</strong> Eating while rushing or multitasking can make it harder to notice fullness and digestion cues.</li>
<li><strong>Take a few calm breaths before meals.</strong> Your digestive system works best when your body is not constantly in “go go go” mode.</li>
<li><strong>Give your body time.</strong> You do not need to eat perfectly, you need to eat consistently.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">Your nervous system is part of your digestion too.</blockquote>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 2<br><em>A calm meal at the table: plate, glass of water, no phone</em></span></div>

<h2>Your skin loves boring consistency</h2>
<p>The skin uses nutrients for repair all the time, so a steady supply matters more than a perfect day:</p>
<table class="tbl"><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Why it matters for skin</th></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Regular meals</em></td><td>Help provide a steady supply of protein, fats, vitamins and minerals your skin uses for repair.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Enough calories</em></td><td>Chronically under-eating can mean fewer resources for hair, skin, hormones and recovery.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Enough protein</em></td><td>Your body needs amino acids to make structural proteins and support tissue renewal.</td></tr>
<tr><td><em class="nm">Dietary fats</em></td><td>Important for cell membranes, hormones and the lipid-rich outer skin barrier.</td></tr></table>
<blockquote class="quote">You cannot “glow” your way out of under-fueling.</blockquote>
<p>If you are restricting a lot or feel anxious about food, please talk to a doctor or registered dietitian. Skin is not worth a restrictive diet. I go deeper on what to eat in <a href="/notes/foods-and-supplements-for-skin/">my food and supplements guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Hydration is more than drinking a lot of water</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li><strong>Water matters, but so do electrolytes</strong> like sodium and potassium.</li>
<li><strong>Fruits and vegetables help.</strong> They provide water alongside minerals, fibre and antioxidants.</li>
<li><strong>Soups, yogurt, smoothies and cooked foods</strong> count toward hydration too.</li>
<li><strong>Do not force huge amounts of water.</strong> Your needs change with heat, activity, caffeine and your diet.</li>
</ul>
<p>What does the research say? A systematic review of six studies in healthy adults found that drinking more water was linked with a small increase in skin hydration, mostly in people who had been drinking little water before. The authors described the evidence as weak in quantity and quality. So hydration supports normal skin function, but more water does not automatically mean more glow. For dry skin, a good moisturiser does a lot of the work from the outside.</p>
<div class="figrow"><figure class="fig"><img src="/img/smoothie-ingredients.webp" alt="Frozen berries in small bowls, a glass of milk and a pink blender on a kitchen counter" style="height:auto;aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover"><figcaption>Frozen berries, milk and the blender.</figcaption></figure><figure class="fig"><img src="/img/pink-day-bottle.webp" alt="A hand holding a pink Bink Day Bottle water bottle in front of its box" style="height:auto;aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover"><figcaption>My pink water bottle.</figcaption></figure></div>

<h2>A little movement after meals is underrated</h2>
<p>You do not need an intense workout. A gentle 10 to 15 minute walk after a meal may help in a few ways:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>it can support post-meal blood sugar regulation</li>
<li>movement helps normal gut motility</li>
<li>it can reduce that heavy “I need to lie down after eating” feeling for some people</li>
</ul>
<p>The blood sugar part is the best studied. A meta-analysis found that light walking breaks lowered glucose and insulin after eating more than standing did, and trials in people with type 2 diabetes or at risk have found that about 10 to 15 minutes after each meal can improve glucose control as much as, or more than, one longer walk. Those studies are in specific groups, so how much it matters for you is less clear, but it is a free, low-risk habit.</p>
<blockquote class="quote">Your body loves gentle movement more than punishment.</blockquote>
<p>Consistency matters more than intensity here.</p>

<h2>Sleep is secretly a beauty treatment</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Your skin does a lot of repair work while you sleep.</li>
<li>Poor sleep can increase inflammatory signalling and make skin feel more reactive.</li>
<li>Sleep also influences appetite, blood sugar regulation and the gut microbiome.</li>
<li>Even skincare performs better when the basics are there: food, sleep and recovery.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">Your 10-step routine cannot replace 5 missing hours of sleep.</blockquote>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 5<br><em>Bedroom in the evening: soft light, sleep mask, a book</em></span></div>

<h2>Stress: the trigger nobody puts in a supplement bottle</h2>
<p>Stress is one of the biggest skin triggers, and no supplement fixes it. As I understand it, stress can:</p>
<ul class="chk">
<li>change gut motility and digestion</li>
<li>influence inflammatory pathways</li>
<li>make acne, redness, itching or sensitivity feel worse in some people</li>
</ul>
<p>A review of 21 studies describes how psychological stress activates stress hormones that reduce the skin’s barrier lipids and hydration and increase water loss. Rest, boundaries, sleep and slower routines are part of wellness too. Sometimes the most supportive thing you can do for your skin is make your life feel a little less overwhelming. I cover the cortisol link in <a href="/notes/foods-that-cause-breakouts/">my note on hidden skin triggers</a>.</p>

<h2>Gut-friendly does not mean eating “cleaner and cleaner”</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>More restriction is not always better.</li>
<li>Your microbiome generally benefits from variety, not perfection.</li>
<li>Different plant foods provide different fibres and polyphenols.</li>
<li>You can still eat bread, dessert, pasta and restaurant food and have a nourishing diet.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Try adding before subtracting:</strong> berries, oats, seeds, herbs, vegetables, legumes and fermented foods, whatever your body tolerates. Data from the American Gut Project, a large citizen-science study, found that people who ate around 30 different plant foods a week had more diverse gut microbes than people who ate 10 or fewer. Herbs, spices, nuts and seeds count, so it is more achievable than it sounds.</p>
<div class="ph fig3"><span>Image 6<br><em>Colourful plant foods: figs, berries, oats, seeds, vegetables</em></span></div>

<h2>My healthy gut and glowing skin checklist</h2>
<ul class="chk">
<li>Eat enough</li>
<li>Get protein daily</li>
<li>Eat plants in different colours</li>
<li>Include healthy fats</li>
<li>Drink according to thirst and activity</li>
<li>Walk and move regularly</li>
<li>Sleep properly</li>
<li>Stop changing your entire routine every 3 days</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="quote">Healthy skin and digestion are usually built from boring little habits repeated for months.</blockquote>

<h2>How I would start: one habit a week</h2>
<p>Doing all eight at once is how habits fall apart. I would add one at a time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Week 1:</strong> regular meals with protein.</li>
<li><strong>Week 2:</strong> a gentle 10 minute walk after your biggest meal.</li>
<li><strong>Week 3:</strong> add one new plant food or colour a day.</li>
<li><strong>Week 4:</strong> protect your sleep window and a calm few minutes before bed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Then keep what feels easy and give it a few months before you judge the result.</p>

<div class="callout"><div class="up">When I would see a doctor</div><p>If you have ongoing digestive symptoms such as pain, bloating, blood in the stool or unexplained weight loss, or persistent skin problems, please see a doctor or dermatologist. I am not a medical professional, and this note is not a substitute for one.</p></div>

